QB.add(21,[{"n":1,"q":"In Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus, what characterizes stage 3 of beta-cell destruction?","o":[{"t":"Symptomatic hyperglycemia"},{"t":"Dysglycemic symptomatic"},{"t":"Normoglycemic symptomatic"},{"t":"Dysglycemic asymptomatic"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Symptomatic hyperglycemia :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus (DM), the progression of beta-cell destruction typically occurs in stages: Stage 1: Autoimmune destruction of beta cells, but no symptoms are present. Normal blood glucose levels. Stage 2: Progressive beta-cell destruction with dysglycemia (impaired glucose metabolism) but no symptoms of hyperglycemia yet. Stage 3: Significant beta-cell destruction leading to symptomatic hyperglycemia. Patients will present with symptoms such as polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss due to elevated blood glucose levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stage 1: Autoimmune destruction of beta cells, but no symptoms are present. Normal blood glucose levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stage 2: Progressive beta-cell destruction with dysglycemia (impaired glucose metabolism) but no symptoms of hyperglycemia yet."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stage 3: Significant beta-cell destruction leading to symptomatic hyperglycemia. Patients will present with symptoms such as polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss due to elevated blood glucose levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"At Stage 3, the destruction is advanced enough that insulin production is insufficient to maintain normal glucose levels, resulting in symptomatic hyperglycemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stage 1: Autoimmune destruction of beta cells, but no symptoms are present. Normal blood glucose levels."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stage 2: Progressive beta-cell destruction with dysglycemia (impaired glucose metabolism) but no symptoms of hyperglycemia yet."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stage 3: Significant beta-cell destruction leading to symptomatic hyperglycemia. Patients will present with symptoms such as polyuria, polydipsia, and weight loss due to elevated blood glucose levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Dysglycemic symptomatic: Dysglycemia refers to abnormal glucose levels, which could be either high (hyperglycemia) or low (hypoglycemia). In Type 1 DM, Stage 3 is characterized specifically by symptomatic hyperglycemia, not just dysglycemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Normoglycemic symptomatic: Normoglycemic means normal blood glucose levels. In Stage 3 of Type 1 DM, the patient is not normoglycemic; instead, they are experiencing symptomatic hyperglycemia due to the loss of beta-cell function."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Dysglycemic asymptomatic: Dysglycemia asymptomatic implies abnormal glucose levels without symptoms. In Stage 3 of Type 1 DM, patients are symptomatic due to hyperglycemia, so this option does not fit the stage described."}]},{"n":2,"q":"A 45-year-old man presents to the outpatient department with symptoms of headache, renal stones, and abdominal pain. Serum calcium is low and PTH is high. Which of the following hormone and associated disease might be causing these symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Parathyroid and Primary hyperparathyroidism"},{"t":"Catecholamines and Pheochromocytoma"},{"t":"Thyroxine and Hypothyroidism"},{"t":"Mineral and bone disorders"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Mineral and bone disorders:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Low calcium levels and elevated PTH levels indicate Mineral and Bone Disorders. In chronic kidney disease-mineral and bone disorder (CKD-MBD), essential changes encompass hyperphosphatemia, hypocalcemia, deficient serum vitamin D levels, and heightened secretion of parathyroid hormone (PTH), culminating in secondary hyperparathyroidism (SHPT)."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Parathyroid and Primary hyperparathyroidism: In primary hyperparathyroidism, there is an elevation in serum calcium levels, which contrasts with the decreased levels seen in the presented case. Additionally, episodic headaches are a common symptom in this condition."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Catecholamines and Pheochromocytoma: Pheochromocytoma typically presents with hypertension and diaphoresis, symptoms not mentioned in the provided case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Thyroxine and Hypothyroidism: Hyperthyroidism typically presents with symptoms such as weight gain and cold intolerance, which contrast with those described in the provided case."}]},{"n":3,"q":"A 45-year-old man presents to the outpatient department with proximal muscle weakness, purple stretch marks, obesity, and easy bruising. What is the underlying condition?","o":[{"t":"Hyperthyroidism"},{"t":"Hypercortisolism"},{"t":"Hyperparathyroidism"},{"t":"Hyperaldosteronism"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Hypercortisolism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presented scenario suggests Cushing syndrome with features like weight gain, purple striae, and proximal myopathy. Hypercortisolism is the underlying condition in Cushing syndrome. It's primarily caused by chronic over-production of cortisol by the adrenal glands, often due to adrenal or pituitary tumors. Pituitary-dependent cortisol excess, termed Cushing’s disease, accounts for about 80% of cases of Cushing syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, C & D are incorrect."}]},{"n":4,"q":"All of the following are criteria for diagnosis of diabetes ketoacidosis, except?","o":[{"t":"Serum glucose of more than 250 mg"},{"t":"Anion gap of more than 10-12"},{"t":"Serum bicarbonate less than 15 mmol"},{"t":"Serum potassium less than 3.5 mEq"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Serum potassium less than 3.5 mEq:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Serum potassium below 3.5 mEq can occur in diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) but isn't part of diagnostic criteria. In DKA, patients are often potassium-depleted, yet plasma potassium levels may not fully indicate a total deficit. Initially, levels might even be elevated due to factors like water loss and potassium shifting. However, treatment can lead to a rapid drop in plasma potassium due to fluid dilution, insulin-induced movement into cells, and ongoing renal loss."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Serum glucose of more than 250 mg: Serum glucose above 250 mg/dL is a diagnostic criterion for DKA. This leads to osmotic diuresis, causing dehydration and electrolyte loss, especially sodium and potassium."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Anion gap of more than 10-12: An anion gap above 10-12 is a diagnostic criterion for DKA. It occurs because bicarbonate is replaced by b-hydroxybutyric acid and acetoacetic acid, reducing the sum of bicarbonate and chloride concentrations and raising the anion gap."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Serum potassium less than 3.5 mEq: Serum bicarbonate below 15 mmol/L is a diagnostic criterion for DKA. Ketosis causes blood ketone accumulation, resulting in metabolic acidosis. This shift forces hydrogen ions into cells, displacing potassium ions."}]},{"n":5,"q":"Which of the following tests is the best for assessing long-term control of diabetes mellitus?","o":[{"t":"Glycosylated Hemoglobin"},{"t":"Glycated Albumin"},{"t":"Serum Fructosamine"},{"t":"Urine Sugar"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Glycosylated Hemoglobin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This test is the most reliable for evaluating long-term glucose control in individuals with diabetes. It measures the percentage of hemoglobin that is glycosylated, reflecting average blood glucose levels over the past 2-3 months."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Glycated Albumin : This test is useful for assessing short-term glycemic control. It measures the percentage of albumin that is glycated and reflects average blood glucose levels over the past 2-3 weeks."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Serum Fructosamine : This test, also known as glycated albumin, provides information similar to glycated albumin and is used to monitor short-term changes in glucose control."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Urine Sugar: This test measures the amount of glucose in the urine. It is less reliable for assessing long-term glucose control because it can be influenced by factors such as renal threshold and hydration status."}]},{"n":6,"q":"Which of the following is the diagnostic test for carcinoid tumors?","o":[{"t":"24-hour urinary catecholamines"},{"t":"Serum calcitonin levels"},{"t":"Serum chromogranin A"},{"t":"Urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Urinary 5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Carcinoid tumors are neuroendocrine tumors that secrete serotonin. Serotonin is metabolized into 5-HIAA, which is excreted in the urine. Elevated levels of 5-HIAA in a 24-hour urine sample are indicative of a carcinoid tumor."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and C are not diagnostic of carcinoid tumors."}]},{"n":7,"q":"A patient presents with hypotension, hyponatremia and blackening of the knuckles and perioral area. Which of the following conditions is most likely associated with these symptoms?","o":[{"t":"Conn syndrome"},{"t":"Cushing's syndrome"},{"t":"Primary ACTH deficiency"},{"t":"Addison’s disease"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Addison’s Disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Addison’s Disease : Symptoms : Hypotension : Due to insufficient cortisol production affecting blood pressure. Hyponatremia : Caused by low aldosterone levels, leading to reduced sodium reabsorption. Hyperpigmentation : Characterized by darkening of the skin, including knuckles and perioral areas, due to increased ACTH levels stimulating melanocytes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms : Hypotension : Due to insufficient cortisol production affecting blood pressure. Hyponatremia : Caused by low aldosterone levels, leading to reduced sodium reabsorption. Hyperpigmentation : Characterized by darkening of the skin, including knuckles and perioral areas, due to increased ACTH levels stimulating melanocytes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension : Due to insufficient cortisol production affecting blood pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyponatremia : Caused by low aldosterone levels, leading to reduced sodium reabsorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperpigmentation : Characterized by darkening of the skin, including knuckles and perioral areas, due to increased ACTH levels stimulating melanocytes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Addison’s Disease :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms : Hypotension : Due to insufficient cortisol production affecting blood pressure. Hyponatremia : Caused by low aldosterone levels, leading to reduced sodium reabsorption. Hyperpigmentation : Characterized by darkening of the skin, including knuckles and perioral areas, due to increased ACTH levels stimulating melanocytes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension : Due to insufficient cortisol production affecting blood pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyponatremia : Caused by low aldosterone levels, leading to reduced sodium reabsorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperpigmentation : Characterized by darkening of the skin, including knuckles and perioral areas, due to increased ACTH levels stimulating melanocytes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Symptoms :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypotension : Due to insufficient cortisol production affecting blood pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyponatremia : Caused by low aldosterone levels, leading to reduced sodium reabsorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hyperpigmentation : Characterized by darkening of the skin, including knuckles and perioral areas, due to increased ACTH levels stimulating melanocytes."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypotension : Due to insufficient cortisol production affecting blood pressure."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hyponatremia : Caused by low aldosterone levels, leading to reduced sodium reabsorption."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hyperpigmentation : Characterized by darkening of the skin, including knuckles and perioral areas, due to increased ACTH levels stimulating melanocytes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Conn Syndrome: Characterized by primary hyperaldosteronism, which typically leads to hypertension, hypokalemia (low potassium), and metabolic alkalosis, but not usually associated with hyperpigmentation or hyponatremia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cushing’s Syndrome: Results from excess cortisol production and can present with hypertension, hyperglycemia, and hypokalemia. Hyperpigmentation is not a common feature; instead, you might see features such as easy bruising and a “moon facies.”"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Primary ACTH Deficiency: Leads to low cortisol levels but not usually associated with hyperpigmentation. Patients may experience symptoms of adrenal insufficiency, but hyperpigmentation is more characteristic of primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison’s disease) rather than ACTH deficiency."}]},{"n":8,"q":"Patient present with large sweaty hands, macroglossia and frontal bossing. Select the best test for confirmation of the diagnosis:","o":[{"t":"GHRH levels"},{"t":"IGF- 1"},{"t":"IGF- 2"},{"t":"GH levels after glucose suppression"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - IGF-1:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The best test for confirming a diagnosis of acromegaly (often caused by excess growth hormone) is measuring Insulin-like Growth Factor 1 (IGF-1) . IGF-1 levels are elevated in acromegaly due to the increased secretion of growth hormone. IGF-1 is a stable marker that reflects chronic exposure to growth hormone and is therefore more reliable for diagnosis compared to intermittent measurements of growth hormone."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - GHRH levels: Growth Hormone-Releasing Hormone (GHRH) levels are not typically used for the diagnosis of acromegaly. While GHRH can stimulate growth hormone release, its levels are not directly indicative of acromegaly."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - IGF-2: IGF-2 is not typically used for diagnosing acromegaly. It is more relevant in conditions like certain tumors and is not a primary diagnostic marker for acromegaly."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - GH levels after glucose suppression: While glucose suppression tests are used to evaluate growth hormone suppression, measuring GH levels after glucose suppression is less reliable than IGF-1 for diagnosing acromegaly. IGF-1 provides a more consistent indication of growth hormone excess."}]},{"n":9,"q":"In a patient with type 1 diabetes at the 3rd stage of beta cell damage, which of the following conditions is most likely to be observed?","o":[{"t":"Autoimmune positive + normoglycemia"},{"t":"Autoimmune negative + normoglycemia"},{"t":"Autoimmune positive + dysglycemia"},{"t":"Autoimmune negative + dysglycemia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Autoimmune positive + dysglycemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In Stage 3 of type 1 diabetes, there is significant beta cell damage due to an ongoing autoimmune process, leading to dysglycemia (impaired glucose levels). At this stage, there is insufficient insulin production to maintain normal glucose levels, causing hyperglycemia, which characterizes dysglycemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Autoimmune positive + normoglycemia: This situation is typically seen in the early stages of type 1 diabetes (Stage 1), where there is an autoimmune response against beta cells, but blood glucose levels remain normal because there is still enough insulin production to maintain glucose homeostasis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Autoimmune negative + normoglycemia: This scenario is not associated with type 1 diabetes. In type 1 diabetes, the autoimmune process is the hallmark feature. If there are no autoimmune markers (autoantibodies), it suggests that type 1 diabetes is unlikely. Normoglycemia in such a context would also not indicate any beta cell damage typical of type 1 diabetes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Autoimmune negative + dysglycemia: This condition is more suggestive of type 2 diabetes or other forms of diabetes where autoimmune destruction of beta cells is not present. Dysglycemia without autoimmunity does not fit with the progression of type 1 diabetes, which is defined by the presence of autoimmune markers."}]},{"n":10,"q":"A patient presents with features of hyperthyroidism, characterized by decreased TSH levels and elevated free T3 and T4. Which of the following statements about this condition is NOT true?","o":[{"t":"The condition will improve simultaneously with improvement in the thyrotoxic state."},{"t":"In 10% of cases, the condition is present without hyperthyroidism."},{"t":"Severe disease can cause vision loss."},{"t":"No specs criteria is used for this condition."}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - The condition will improve simultaneously with improvement in the thyrotoxic state:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This statement is not true because the condition being referred to is likely Graves' ophthalmopathy (thyroid eye disease), which does not always improve simultaneously with the resolution of the thyrotoxic state. The eye manifestations can persist or even worsen independently of the thyroid hormone levels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"This statement is not true because the condition being referred to is likely Graves' ophthalmopathy (thyroid eye disease), which does not always improve simultaneously with the resolution of the thyrotoxic state. The eye manifestations can persist or even worsen independently of the thyroid hormone levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options B, C and D are true statements regarding hyperthyroidism and Graves' ophthalmopathy."}],"qi":[5455]},{"n":11,"q":"A young woman presents with weight loss, tachycardia, heat intolerance, and tremors. On examination, she has exophthalmos. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Parkinson's disease"},{"t":"Graves' disease"},{"t":"Addison's disease"},{"t":"Alzheimer's disease"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Graves' disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Graves' disease is an autoimmune disorder that leads to hyperthyroidism. Symptoms such as weight loss, tachycardia, heat intolerance, and tremors are all characteristic of an overactive thyroid. Exophthalmos (protrusion of the eyes) is a hallmark feature of Graves' disease, distinguishing it from other thyroid conditions. This makes Graves' disease the most likely diagnosis given the patient's symptoms and findings."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Parkinson's disease: Parkinson's disease is a neurodegenerative disorder characterized by tremors, bradykinesia (slowness of movement), rigidity, and postural instability. It does not typically present with weight loss, heat intolerance, or exophthalmos, which are more suggestive of a hyperthyroid state."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Addison's disease: Addison's disease is a disorder of adrenal insufficiency characterized by symptoms such as fatigue, weight loss, hyperpigmentation, hypotension, and salt cravings. It does not typically present with tachycardia, heat intolerance, tremors, or exophthalmos, which are indicative of hyperthyroidism rather than adrenal insufficiency."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Alzheimer's disease: Alzheimer's disease is a neurodegenerative condition that primarily affects memory and cognitive function. It does not cause weight loss, tachycardia, heat intolerance, tremors, or exophthalmos, which are not associated with its clinical presentation."}]},{"n":12,"q":"A patient with a history of Wilson's disease presents with neurological manifestations. Which of the following is the best diagnostic modality?","o":[{"t":"Serum ceruloplasmin"},{"t":"Urinary copper excretion"},{"t":"Hepatic parenchymal demonstration of copper"},{"t":"Anti-tissue transglutaminase (Anti-tTG) antibody"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Hepatic parenchymal demonstration of copper:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Wilson's disease is a genetic disorder characterized by excessive accumulation of copper in various body tissues, primarily the liver and brain. The best diagnostic modality to confirm Wilson's disease, especially in the presence of neurological manifestations, is hepatic parenchymal demonstration of copper . This is usually done by performing a liver biopsy to measure the hepatic copper concentration, which is considered the gold standard for diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Serum ceruloplasmin: While serum ceruloplasmin is often low in Wilson's disease, it is not the most reliable diagnostic tool, as ceruloplasmin levels can be low in other conditions and may be normal in some patients with Wilson's disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Urinary copper excretion: This test measures 24-hour urinary copper excretion and is elevated in Wilson's disease, but it is not as definitive as hepatic copper measurement. It is useful as part of the diagnostic workup but not the best single test."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Anti-tissue transglutaminase (Anti-tTG) antibody: This antibody is associated with celiac disease and is not relevant to the diagnosis of Wilson's disease."}]},{"n":13,"q":"Pheochromocytoma is associated in production of all of the below except.","o":[{"t":"Epinephrine"},{"t":"Norepinephrine"},{"t":"Cortisol"},{"t":"Dopamine"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Cortisol:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cortisol: Pheochromocytomas are not associated with cortisol production. Cortisol is primarily produced by the adrenal cortex in response to adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH) stimulation, whereas pheochromocytomas originate from the adrenal medulla and are more focused on the production of catecholamines"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and D:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pheochromocytomas are associated with production of these hormones."}]},{"n":14,"q":"In an asymptomatic patient, which of the following is the most appropriate diagnostic criteria for diabetes mellitus based on the ADA 2023 guidelines?","o":[{"t":"Oral glucose tolerance test : 2 hrs value >200 mg/dL"},{"t":"HbA1C ‡ 5.9%"},{"t":"Fasting plasma glucose >5mmol/L"},{"t":"2 hrs plasma glucose >7 mmol/L"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Oral glucose tolerance test: 2 hrs value >200 mg/dL:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ADA 2023: Diagnostic criteria of diabetes mellitus for asymptomatic is HbA1C ‡ 6.5% (as per NGSP) Fasting plasma glucose >126 mg/dL (>7 mmol/L) Oral glucose tolerance test: 75 g of anhydrous glucose 2 hrs value >200 mg/dL (>11.1mmol/L)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HbA1C ‡ 6.5% (as per NGSP)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fasting plasma glucose >126 mg/dL (>7 mmol/L)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral glucose tolerance test: 75 g of anhydrous glucose 2 hrs value >200 mg/dL (>11.1mmol/L)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"2 hrs value >200 mg/dL (>11.1mmol/L)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HbA1C ‡ 6.5% (as per NGSP)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fasting plasma glucose >126 mg/dL (>7 mmol/L)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Oral glucose tolerance test: 75 g of anhydrous glucose 2 hrs value >200 mg/dL (>11.1mmol/L)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"2 hrs value >200 mg/dL (>11.1mmol/L)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"2 hrs value >200 mg/dL (>11.1mmol/L)"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options B,C and D are incorrect as given above"}]},{"n":15,"q":"What is the condition most commonly associated with elevated TSH levels and decreased T4 levels?","o":[{"t":"Hypoparathyroidism"},{"t":"Pituitary Adenoma"},{"t":"Grave's disease"},{"t":"Hashimoto's thyroiditis"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Hashimoto's thyroiditis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hashimoto's thyroiditis is an autoimmune disorder where the body's immune system mistakenly attacks the thyroid gland, leading to chronic inflammation and destruction of thyroid tissue."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This results in decreased production of thyroid hormones, including T4. As a compensatory mechanism, the pituitary gland releases more TSH to stimulate the thyroid gland, causing increased TSH levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Hypoparathyroidism: Hypoparathyroidism is a condition characterized by inadequate production or secretion of parathyroid hormone (PTH) by the parathyroid glands."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Pituitary Adenoma: While it can cause various hormonal imbalances, such as excessive production of prolactin (leading to hyperprolactinemia), it does not typically result in increased TSH and low T4 levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Grave's disease: In Grave's disease, TSH levels are typically decreased due to the negative feedback mechanism resulting from high levels of thyroid hormones."}]},{"n":16,"q":"Mark the true statements? 1. MEN 2B is also known as Sipple syndrome 2. MEN 1 is also known as Wermer syndrome 3. MEN 2B is characterized by the presence of Marfanoid Habitus. 4. MEN 4 is a/w CDKN1B","o":[{"t":"1 and 2 are true"},{"t":"3 and 4 are true"},{"t":"1, 2 and 4 are true"},{"t":"2, 3 and 4 are true"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - 3 and 4 are true:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MEN 1 is also known as Wermer syndrome: This statement is TRUE. MEN-1 or Wermer syndrome is caused by defect in chromosome 11q13. premature aging."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marfanoid Habitus is a feature of MEN 2B: This statement is true. Marfanoid habitus, which refers to the skeletal features similar to Marfan syndrome, is commonly seen in individuals with MEN 2B."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MEN 4 is associated with CDKN1B: This statement is true. MEN 4 is a rare form of multiple endocrine neoplasia that is associated with mutations in the CDKN1B gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, C, and D"}]},{"n":17,"q":"What is the disease depicted in the image below?","o":[{"t":"Acromegaly"},{"t":"PCOS"},{"t":"Cushing’s syndrome"},{"t":"Advanced obesity"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Cushing’s syndrome:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The above image is that of Cushing’s syndrome which results from chronic exposure to glucocorticoids."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"OptionA - Acromegaly is due to excess growth hormone and symptoms include enlargement of face, hands, and feet."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - PCOS is an hyper estrogenic state with presentation of the irregular menstrual cycle, acne and hirsutism."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - This is not the case of advanced obesity."}],"qi":[5459]},{"n":18,"q":"What is a macrovascular complication that develops in the advanced stages of diabetes?","o":[{"t":"Coronary artery disease"},{"t":"Foot ulcer"},{"t":"Nephropathy"},{"t":"Retinopathy"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Coronary artery disease:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Coronary artery disease is a macrovascular complication of diabetes, while others are all microvascular complications."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It increases the cardiovascular mortality due to atherosclerosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Foot ulcer is a microvascular complication of diabetes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Nephropathy is a microvascular complication of diabetes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Retinopathy is a microvascular complication of diabetes."}]},{"n":19,"q":"What is the treatment for hypercalcemia caused by vitamin D toxicity?","o":[{"t":"Dexamethasone"},{"t":"Hydroxychloroquine"},{"t":"Chloroquine"},{"t":"Ketoconazole"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Dexamethasone:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dexamethasone: Dexamethasone is a corticosteroid that can be used in the treatment of hypercalcemia due to vitamin D toxicity. It works by reducing intestinal calcium absorption and promoting renal calcium excretion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hydroxychloroquine: Hydroxychloroquine main uses are in the treatment of malaria and certain autoimmune disorders such as rheumatoid arthritis and lupus."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Chloroquine: Similar to hydroxychloroquine, chloroquine is an antimalarial drug and is not commonly used in the treatment of hypercalcemia due to vitamin D toxicity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Ketoconazole: Ketoconazole is primarily used in the treatment of fungal infections."}]},{"n":20,"q":"Which condition is frequently linked to Addison's disease?","o":[{"t":"Autoimmune adrenalitis"},{"t":"Adrenocortical carcinoma"},{"t":"Hypernephroma"},{"t":"Medullary carcinoma thyroid"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Autoimmune adrenalitis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This is the correct answer. Addison's disease, also known as primary adrenal insufficiency, is commonly associated with autoimmune adrenalitis. In this condition, the body's immune system mistakenly attacks and destroys the adrenal cortex, leading to inadequate production of adrenal hormones."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Adrenocortical carcinoma - Adrenocortical carcinoma is a rare malignant tumor of the adrenal cortex. While it can cause adrenal hormone imbalances, it is not commonly associated with Addison's disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypernephroma - Hypernephroma is an outdated term for renal cell carcinoma, which is a type of kidney cancer. It is unrelated to Addison's disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Medullary carcinoma thyroid - Medullary carcinoma of the thyroid is a type of thyroid cancer that arises from the parafollicular C cells. It is not associated with Addison's disease."}]},{"n":21,"q":"A 56-year-old woman presented with a gradually diminishing visual field. She was found to have a pituitary mass and underwent trans-sphenoidal hypophysectomy. Now she has low levels of ACTH, TSH, FSH and LH. Which of the following hormone supplements is not needed in the patient postoperatively?","o":[{"t":"Mineralocorticoids"},{"t":"Thyroid hormones"},{"t":"Glucocorticoids"},{"t":"Estradiol"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Mineralocorticoids:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mineralocorticoids, such as aldosterone, are responsible for regulating electrolyte and water balance in the body. They are not produced by the pituitary gland but rather by the adrenal cortex."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, the patient's deficiency in ACTH (adrenocorticotropic hormone) will not directly affect mineralocorticoid production. Hence, mineralocorticoid supplementation is not needed in this case."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Thyroid hormones: Thyroid hormones, including TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone), play a crucial role in regulating metabolism. As the patient has low levels of TSH, indicating thyroid hormone deficiency, thyroid hormone supplementation is necessary to maintain normal thyroid function."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Glucocorticoids: Glucocorticoids, such as cortisol, are produced by the adrenal cortex under the stimulation of ACTH. With the deficiency of ACTH, the production of glucocorticoids is reduced. Therefore, glucocorticoid supplementation is needed to replace the deficient cortisol and provide the necessary physiological effects."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Estradiol: Estradiol is a female sex hormone produced primarily by the ovaries. The deficiency of FSH (follicle-stimulating hormone) and LH (luteinizing hormone) indicates dysfunction of the gonadotropins, which regulate the production of estradiol. Consequently, estradiol supplementation may be required to maintain hormonal balance in premenopausal women."}]},{"n":22,"q":"Please calculate the sodium deficit in the patient, who weighs 60 kg, using the provided serum electrolyte values: pH of 7.42, sodium (Na+) level of 120 mEq/L, and serum chloride (CI-) level of 90 mEq/L.","o":[{"t":"20 mEq"},{"t":"200 mEq"},{"t":"400 mEq"},{"t":"720 mEq"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - 720 mEq:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To calculate the sodium deficit in the patient, we need to determine the patient's total body water (TBW) and current sodium content, and then calculate the difference between the actual sodium content and the desired sodium level."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The formula to calculate sodium deficit is: Sodium Deficit (mEq) = TBW (L) × (Desired Na+ - Current Na+)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sodium Deficit (mEq) = TBW (L) × (Desired Na+ - Current Na+)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"To estimate the TBW, we can use the following formula: TBW (L) = 0.6 × body weight (kg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TBW (L) = 0.6 × body weight (kg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Given that the patient weighs 60 kg, the TBW would be: TBW (L) = 0.6 × 60 kg = 36 L"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TBW (L) = 0.6 × 60 kg = 36 L"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The desired Na+ level is typically around 140 mEq/L. Using the given values, we can calculate the sodium deficit:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Sodium Deficit (mEq) = TBW (L) × (Desired Na+ - Current Na+)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TBW (L) = 0.6 × body weight (kg)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TBW (L) = 0.6 × 60 kg = 36 L"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Sodium Deficit (mEq) = 36 L × (140 mEq/L - 120 mEq/L) Sodium Deficit (mEq)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"= 36 L × 20 mEq/L Sodium Deficit (mEq)"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"= 720 mEq"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Therefore, the correct option is D, \"720 mEq.\""},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, B, and C: These values do not account for the TBW calculation or the difference between the desired and current sodium levels."}]},{"n":23,"q":"What is the interpretation of elevated serum Calcium levels in a patient with normal SAP, PTH, and Vitamin D3 levels?","o":[{"t":"Vitamin D intoxication"},{"t":"Hyperparathyroidism"},{"t":"Multiple myeloma"},{"t":"Nutritional rickets"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Multiple myeloma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Multiple myeloma : Multiple myeloma is a type of cancer that involves plasma cells in the bone marrow. It can lead to the excessive production of monoclonal immunoglobulins (M-protein), which can cause hypercalcemia. In multiple myeloma, the s erum calcium levels can be elevated while SAP, PTH, and vitamin D3 levels remain normal."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Vitamin D intoxication: In cases of vitamin D intoxication, both serum calcium and vitamin D3 levels are typically elevated. The PTH levels are usually suppressed due to the feedback mechanism between vitamin D and PTH."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hyperparathyroidism: Hyperparathyroidism is characterized by increased PTH levels, which stimulate the release of calcium from bone, resulting in elevated serum calcium levels. In this case, since PTH levels are normal, hyperparathyroidism is not the likely diagnosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Nutritional rickets: This option is incorrect. Nutritional rickets is a condition caused by a deficiency of vitamin D, calcium, or phosphate. In this case, serum calcium levels would typically be decreased rather than elevated."}]},{"n":24,"q":"Tumor Lysis Syndrome is an oncological emergency presenting with all of the below except.","o":[{"t":"Hypophosphatemia"},{"t":"Hypocalcemia"},{"t":"Hyperuricemia"},{"t":"Hyperkalemia"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Hypophosphatemia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypophosphatemia: During TLS, there is a rapid release of intracellular phosphate from lysed cells , leading to an i ncrease in serum phosphate levels (hyperphosphatemia) rather than a decrease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hypocalcemia: Hypocalcemia is commonly observed in Tumor Lysis Syndrome. The increased phosphate levels caused by tumor lysis can bind to calcium, resulting in a decrease in ionized calcium levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hyperuricemia: Hyperuricemia is a characteristic feature of Tumor Lysis Syndrome. The release of purines from the breakdown of DNA in tumor cells leads to increased uric acid production."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hyperkalemia: Hyperkalemia is frequently seen in Tumor Lysis Syndrome. The release of intracellular potassium from lysed cells, combined with impaired renal excretion, can result in increased serum potassium levels."}]},{"n":25,"q":"What is the main physical finding of prolactinoma that follows galactorrhea in a 19-year-old nulligravida woman who has been experiencing milk discharge from her nipples for the last 5 months and is diagnosed with prolactinoma?","o":[{"t":"Bitemporal hemianopia"},{"t":"Anovulatory cycles"},{"t":"Amenorrhea"},{"t":"Infertility"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Bitemporal hemianopia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Prolactinomas are benign pituitary tumors that secrete excessive prolactin. When large enough, they can compress the optic chiasm, where optic nerve fibers partially cross. This pressure disrupts the outer (temporal) visual fields of both eyes, leading to bitemporal hemianopia—a condition where patients lose peripheral vision but retain central vision."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Bitemporal hemianopia is a key physical finding in large prolactinomas and often promptsfurther diagnostic imaging to confirm the tumor's presence."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Anovulatory cycles- While prolactinomas can disrupt normal menstrual cycles, leading to anovulatory cycles, it is not the characteristic physical finding after galactorrhea."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Amenorrhea- Prolactinomas can cause hormonal imbalances, including elevated prolactin levels, which can disrupt normal menstrual cycles and lead to amenorrhea. However, it is not the characteristic physical finding after galactorrhea."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D : Infertility- Prolactinomas can disrupt normal hormonal balance and interfere with ovulation, leading to difficulties in conceiving. However, infertility is not the characteristic physical finding after galactorrhea"}]},{"n":26,"q":"Which of the following has a slow onset of action?","o":[{"t":"Glargine"},{"t":"Lispro"},{"t":"Regular"},{"t":"NPH"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Glargine insulin is a long-acting insulin that is characterized by a slow onset of action, typically taking about 1 to 2 hours to begin working. It is designed to provide a steady release of insulin over an extended period (approximately 24 hours). This slow onset and prolonged duration make it suitable for providing basal insulin coverage."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Lispro insulin is a rapid-acting insulin that has a quick onset of action, typically within 15 minutes of administration. It is used to control postprandial glucose spikes after meals. Unlike glargine, lispro insulin has a fast onset rather than a slow onset."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Regular insulin, also known as short-acting insulin, has an onset of action of approximately 30 minutes. It is used for mealtime coverage or in combination with long-acting insulin for basal coverage. It is not characterized by a slow onset like glargine."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D : NPH (Neutral Protamine Hagedorn) insulin is an intermediate-acting insulin. It has a slower onset compared to rapid-acting insulins but is not specifically known for having a slow onset like glargine. NPH insulin typically takes about 1 to 2 hours to start working"}]},{"n":27,"q":"Which type of insulin should be administered to manage a 32-year-old male patient with a medical history of peptic ulcer disease and diabetes mellitus, who presents to the emergency department complaining of nausea, abdominal pain, and lethargy for the past two days? The patient also reports occasional dark, tarry stools. Laboratory tests indicate elevated blood glucose levels and a high anion gap metabolic acidosis on an arterial blood gas sample.","o":[{"t":"Regular insulin"},{"t":"Lispro"},{"t":"Glargine"},{"t":"Aspart"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Regular insulin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular insulin, also known as short-acting insulin , is the most appropriate choice for managing DKA."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular insulin is the correct choice because it has an onset of action of approximately 30 minutes, peaks in 2 to 4 hours , and lasts for about 6 to 8 hours. Its slower onset and longer duration of a ction make it suitable for managing the prolonged hyperglycemia and metabolic acidosis associated with DKA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Lispro insulin is a rapid-acting insulin with a quick onset of action but a shorter duration compared to regular insulin. It is commonly used to manage postprandial glucose spikes in individuals with diabetes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Glargine insulin is a long-acting insulin that provides a steady release of insulin over an extended period (approximately 24 hours). It is used for basal insulin coverage. ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Aspart insulin is a rapid-acting insulin similar to lispro insulin. It has a rapid onset and shorter duration of action, making it less suitable for the management of DKA"}]},{"n":28,"q":"A diabetic patient came to the outpatient department with complaints of fatigue and frequent urination. On examination vitals are stable and an HbA1c is done. What is the cut-off percentage used for determining controlled diabetes?","o":[{"t":"6.0"},{"t":"6.5"},{"t":"7.0"},{"t":"7.5"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - 7.0:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The cut-off percentage used to determine controlled diabetes is HbA 1 C of <7.0% (<8.0% in elderly frail patients)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment goals for adults with diabetes"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Index Goal"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Glycemic control HbA 1c <7.0%"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Preprandial capillary plasma glucose 80-130 mg/dL Index Goal"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Postprandial capillary plasma <180 mg/dL glucose"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"Blood pressure <140/90 mmHg"}]},{"n":29,"q":"Which of the following is associated with MEN-1 syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Parathyroid adenoma"},{"t":"Parotid tumor"},{"t":"Renal cell carcinoma"},{"t":"Pheochromocytoma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Parathyroid adenoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MEN-1/Wermer syndrome involves the MEN-1 gene mutation ( chromosome 11q13) responsible for the development of menin causing uncontrolled mitosis in the endocrine glands."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"MEN-1 syndrome is associated with:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parathyroid adenoma (most common manifestation)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancreatic adenoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pituitary adenoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Parotid tumor:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parotid tumor is not associated with multiple endocrine neoplasias"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Renal cell carcinoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal cell carcinoma may or may not be associated with MEN-4 syndrome"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Pheochromocytoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pheochromocytoma is associated with MEN-2/ Sipple syndrome."}]},{"n":30,"q":"All of the below are features of Conn’s syndrome except?","o":[{"t":"Metabolic alkalosis"},{"t":"Metabolic acidosis"},{"t":"Hypertension"},{"t":"Muscle cramps"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Metabolic acidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Metabolic acidosis will not be seen in Conn’s syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Conn syndrome has increased levels of aldosterone that stimulate the ENaC channel causing increased sodium and water reabsorption and enhanced urinary loss of potassium and hydrogen ions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Feature of Conn’s syndrome Increased blood pressure due to increased sodium and water reabsorption. Hypokalemic alkalosis due to urinary loss of potassium and hydrogen ions. Polyuria and polydipsia. Right atrium dilatation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased blood pressure due to increased sodium and water reabsorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypokalemic alkalosis due to urinary loss of potassium and hydrogen ions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polyuria and polydipsia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Right atrium dilatation"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased blood pressure due to increased sodium and water reabsorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypokalemic alkalosis due to urinary loss of potassium and hydrogen ions."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Polyuria and polydipsia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Right atrium dilatation"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Metabolic alkalosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This occurs due to the urinary loss of potassium and hydrogen ions"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Hypertension:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This occurs due to increased reabsorption of sodium and water"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Muscle cramps:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This occurs due to hypokalemic alkalosis."}]},{"n":31,"q":"A lung cancer patient has hyperpigmentation of palmar and sole creases. Which of the following is responsible for this presentation?","o":[{"t":"MSH"},{"t":"C-peptide"},{"t":"Neuropeptide Y"},{"t":"Melanin"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - MSH:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ectopic Cushing's syndrome is the most common hormonally induced paraneoplastic syndrome associated with internal malignancy (SCLC ) like lung cancer."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ectopic Cushing's syndrome is caused by the excessive production of proopiomelanocortin (POMC) from tumors located outside of the pituitary gland. POMC serves as a precursor to both melanocyte-stimulating hormone (MSH) and adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH). Both MSH and ACTH can trigger melanin synthesis, leading to the development of hyperpigmentation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - C-peptide:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"C-peptide is produced during the cleavage of proinsulin to form insulin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Neuropeptide Y:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Neuropeptide Y is a hypothalamic peptide that is released during the regulation of appetite."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Melanin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Melanin is responsible for pigmentation of hair and skin and photoprotection of eyes and skin. Depending on the production and transportation of melanin, a syndrome like Waardenburg syndrome, vitiligo could occur."}]},{"n":32,"q":"Most common cause of hypercalcemia in a hospitalized patient is:","o":[{"t":"Malignancy"},{"t":"Parathyroid Adenoma"},{"t":"Parathyroid hyperplasia"},{"t":"Follicular thyroid CA"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A – Malignancy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In malignancy-associated hypercalcemia, the disease is usually not occult; rather, symptoms of malignancy bring the patient to the physician, and hypercalcemia is discovered during the evaluation. In such patients, the interval between detection of hypercalcemia and death, especially without vigorous treatment, is often <6 months. Example- Squamous cell carcinoma of the lungs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Example- Squamous cell carcinoma of the lungs."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Example- Squamous cell carcinoma of the lungs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Parathyroid Adenoma: This option is incorrect as hypercalcemia in an adult who is asymptomatic is usually due to primary hyperparathyroidism."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Parathyroid hyperplasia: This option is incorrect as Hypercalcemia in an adult who is asymptomatic is usually due to primary hyperparathyroidism."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Follicular thyroid CA: Hypervitaminosis D, This option is incorrect as it is iatrogenic."}]},{"n":33,"q":"A young patient developed hyperpigmentation of distal fingers after being hospitalized post adrenalectomy. What could be the possible cause?","o":[{"t":"Addison’s disease"},{"t":"Conn's syndrome"},{"t":"Nelson syndrome"},{"t":"Cushing syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Nelson syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Nelson syndrome is a rare condition that occurs in patients who have had both adrenal glands removed (bilateral adrenalectomy) as a treatment for Cushing's disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is characterized by the development of a pituitary adenoma that produces excess adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), which leads to hyperpigmentation of the skin, especially in areas exposed to friction and pressure, such as the distal fingers."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In patients with Cushing's disease, increased levels of ACTH lead to overproduction of cortisol. Considering the patient has undergone bilateral adrenalectomy, there is an absence of cortisol response to elevated ACTH."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Due to the absent production of cortisol, increased levels of CRH (Corticotropin-releasing hormone) are produced and released from the hypothalamus which in turn activates the pituitary to increases proopiomelanocortin production."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Proopiomelanocortin is a common precursor of MSH (Melanocyte stimulating hormone) and ACTH leading to hyperpigmentation."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Addison’s Disease: Addison's disease involves primary adrenal insufficiency, where the adrenal glands do not produce sufficient steroid hormones. Hyperpigmentation can occur due to increased ACTH levels, but Addison's disease is typically associated with adrenal atrophy or autoimmune destruction, not post-adrenalectomy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Conn's syndrome: Conn's syndrome, or primary hyperaldosteronism, is characterized by excessive production of aldosterone by the adrenal glands. It does not typically cause hyperpigmentation. Symptoms are usually related to hypertension and hypokalemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Cushing syndrome: Cushing syndrome is caused by prolonged exposure to high levels of cortisol. It usually leads to symptoms such as central obesity, moon face, and purple striae, rather than hyperpigmentation of the fingers. Hyperpigmentation can occur if ACTH levels are high, but this is more typical of ectopic ACTH production rather than the syndrome itself."}]},{"n":34,"q":"A 26-year-old female presents with palpitations, weight loss, tremors, sweating and diarrhea. What is the next test that will help in establishing the diagnosis in this patient?","o":[{"t":"TSH and free T4"},{"t":"Anti Thyroid Peroxidase antibodies"},{"t":"Total T3"},{"t":"USG Neck"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - TSH and free T4"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These tests are the primary screening tests for thyroid function."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A low TSH and high free T4 levels would indicate hyperthyroidism, which matches the symptoms described."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"TSH (Thyroid Stimulating Hormone) is produced by the pituitary gland and stimulates the thyroid gland to produce thyroid hormones."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Free T4 is the active form of thyroxine, the main hormone produced by the thyroid gland."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Measuring both TSH and free T4 provides a clear picture of thyroid function. TSH levels are very sensitive to changes in thyroid hormone levels, making it the most reliable initial test for thyroid dysfunction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B. Anti-Thyroid Peroxidase (Anti-TPO) antibodies"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-TPO antibodies are useful in diagnosing autoimmune thyroid diseases like Hashimoto’s thyroiditis or Graves' disease but are not the first-line test for initial diagnosis of hyperthyroidism. They do not provide direct information about thyroid hormone levels or immediate thyroid function."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C. Total T3"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Total T3 can be elevated in hyperthyroidism but is less reliable than free T4 for the initial diagnosis. It can be influenced by factors such as binding proteins and does not provide as comprehensive an assessment as TSH and free T4 levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D. USG Neck"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ultrasound of the neck can help in assessing the thyroid gland’s size, structure, and presence of nodules but does not provide functional information about thyroid hormone levels. It is more useful as a secondary test once thyroid dysfunction is established or to further investigate structural abnormalities."}]},{"n":35,"q":"A child is brought to the ER in an unconscious state after severe vomiting and abdominal pain episodes. HR = 100/min and BP=100/60 mm Hg. work up shows pH=7.2, blood glucose 300 mg/dl, ketone 3+. What should be the best management?","o":[{"t":"Infusion of 0.05 – 0.1 U/kg dose insulin after 1 hour of IVF"},{"t":"0.1 Unit/kg insulin stat followed infusion of 0.5 U- 0.1 U/kg dose insulin and IVF"},{"t":"0.1 U/kg insulin subcutaneous stat followed by IVF"},{"t":"IVF followed by subcutaneous 0.1 U/kg insulin"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Infusion of 0.05 – 0.1 U/kg dose insulin after 1 hour of IVF:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient lab reports and symptoms are suggestive of diabetic ketoacidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patient is vomiting, which implies moderate or greater dehydration without shock."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Start on Normal saline 0.9 % 10 to 20 mL/Kg over 20 to 30 min and correct fluid deficit over next 24 to 48 hours."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patient should also be assesed and treated for hypokalemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Then start continuous IV insulin infusion at 0.05-0.1 unit/kg/hour starting 1 hour after initiation of fluids."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - 0.1 Unit/kg insulin stat followed infusion of 0.5 U- 0.1 U/kg dose insulin and IVF: For pediatric patients with DKA, stat dose of insulin is not recommended. Fluid status and potassium should be corrected before initiating insulin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - 0.1 U/kg insulin subcutaneous stat followed by IVF: Fluid status and potassium should be corrected before initiating insulin."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - IVF followed by subcutaneous 0.1 U/kg insulin: IV fluids followed by an insulin infusion is the most appropriate treatment recommendation."}]},{"n":36,"q":"Which of the following anti diabetic drug is recently approved to be used in patients with heart failure with low ejection fraction and reduced urine output.","o":[{"t":"Linagliptin"},{"t":"Empagliflozin"},{"t":"Pioglitazone"},{"t":"Degludec"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B – Empagliflozin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-diabetic drugs reducing cardiovascular mortality are SGLT2 inhibitors (Empagliflozin, Dapagliflozin) and GLP 1 agonists (Semaglutide, Dulaglutide)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Empagliflozin is an example of an SGLT2 inhibitor"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A – Linagliptin: It is a DPP-4 inhibitor. The DPP 4 enzyme is responsible for the breakdown of incretins such as GLP-1."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C – Pioglitazone: These drugs have been shown to cause dose-dependent fluid retention, hence this drug class is generally avoided in patients with heart failure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D – Degludec: Insulin degludec is a long-acting insulin used to treat hyperglycemia caused by type 1 and 2 diabetes mellitus."}]},{"n":37,"q":"70 year old man presents with low back ache and progressive pallor. On work up, KFT is deranged. LFT is normal with normal level of Albumin, but gamma globulins are disproportionately increased. X ray spine shows multiple lytic lesions. Which of the following is the cell of origin of the diagnosis of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Lymphocyte"},{"t":"Plasma cell"},{"t":"Histiocytes"},{"t":"Monocytes"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Plasma cell:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The clinical presentation of low back ache, progressive pallor, deranged KFT, disproportionately increased gamma globulins, and lytic lesions on X-ray spine is highly suggestive of multiple myeloma, a cancer of plasma cells."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In multiple myeloma, abnormal plasma cells accumulate in the bone marrow and produce excessive amounts of a single type of immunoglobulin, leading to lytic bone lesions and other symptoms."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Lymphocyte: They can give rise to lymphomas, but lymphomas typically present as lymph node enlargements and may involve other organs."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Histiocytes: Diseases involving histiocytes include Langerhans cell histiocytosis and hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis, but these conditions typically present with different clinical features and are not consistent with the provided symptoms and findings."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Monocytes: Monocytes are another type of white blood cell involved in the immune response and can differentiate into macrophages and dendritic cells."}]},{"n":38,"q":"Calcitonin is a marker of which malignancy?","o":[{"t":"Papillary thyroid cancer"},{"t":"Follicular thyroid cancer"},{"t":"Medullary thyroid cancer"},{"t":"Anaplastic thyroid cancer"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Medullary thyroid cancer:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcitonin is a hormone produced by the C-cells (also known as parafollicular cells) of the thyroid gland. Medullary thyroid cancer (MTC) is a type of thyroid cancer that arises from the parafollicular C-cells of the thyroid gland."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcitonin is the tumour marker for medullary thyroid carcinoma"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Papillary thyroid cancer: Papillary thyroid cancer arises from the follicular cells of the thyroid gland, not the parafollicular C-cells."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Follicular thyroid cancer: Similar to papillary thyroid cancer, follicular thyroid cancer also arises from the follicular cells of the thyroid gland and does not usually result in elevated calcitonin levels."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Anaplastic thyroid cancer: Anaplastic thyroid cancer is an aggressive and rare form of thyroid cancer that is not typically associated with elevated calcitonin levels. It arises from the follicular cells of the thyroid gland, similar to papillary and follicular thyroid cancers."}]},{"n":39,"q":"What is the most probable diagnosis for a 30-year-old female patient who reports symptoms of headache, vomiting, absence of menstruation, lactation without pregnancy, decreased sexual desire, acromegaly, and infertility, along with the presence of bitemporal hemianopia during a physical examination?","o":[{"t":"Corticotroph adenoma"},{"t":"Gonadotroph adenoma"},{"t":"Mammosomatotroph adenoma"},{"t":"Glioma"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C: Mammosomatotroph adenoma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above case is that of a mammosomatotroph adenoma of the pituitary which causes an excess of prolactin and growth hormone secretion. Common symptoms include headache, vomiting, amenorrhea, galactorrhoea, loss of libido, acromegaly, and infertility."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Corticotroph adenoma secretes ACTH and has features of Cushing’s."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Gonadotroph adenoma secretes FSH and LH and can present with PCOS and ovarian hypersensitivity syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Gliomas are derived from glial cells and present with headache, nausea, vomiting, and seizures."}]},{"n":40,"q":"What is the most suitable medication for treating a female patient with galactorrhea, who has tested negative for pregnancy and has been diagnosed with a significant pituitary tumor through an MRI, considering her refusal for surgery?","o":[{"t":"Bromocriptine"},{"t":"Promethazine"},{"t":"Octreotide"},{"t":"Clozapine"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A- Bromocriptine:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above given scenario indicates prolactinoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Bromocriptine is a dopamine agonist that increases the level of dopamine leading to inhibition of prolactin."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Other management Cabergoline is a long acting drug used in the treatment of prolactinoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cabergoline is a long acting drug used in the treatment of prolactinoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cabergoline is a long acting drug used in the treatment of prolactinoma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Promethazine : It is an antihistamine drug used to treat allergies, motion sickness , and nausea. It has no role in the situation of pituitary tumors."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Octreotide : It is a somatostatin analog that restricts the discharge of various hormones, containing development hormone and insulin-like growth factor-1. It is used to treat acromegaly and neuroendocrine tumors but has no function in the situation of prolactinomas."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Clozapine : It is an antipsychotic drug used to treat emotional disorders and additional psychiatric disorders. It has no role in the situation of pituitary tumors."}]},{"n":41,"q":"What is the cause of death in pediatric patients with Diabetic Ketoacidosis?","o":[{"t":"Sepsis"},{"t":"Dehydration"},{"t":"Hypokalemia"},{"t":"Cerebral edema"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Cerebral edema:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Death in case of Diabetic Ketoacidosis of a child is \"Cerebral edema.\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral edema refers to the swelling of the brain due to the movement of fluids from the bloodstream into the brain tissue."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cerebral edema ca n increase intracranial pressure, impair brain function, and have potentially fatal consequences ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and C are incorrect. These are the common complications of diabeticketoacidosis."}]},{"n":42,"q":"A high-dose dexamethasone suppression test was done on a chronic smoker with complaints of weight gain and hyperpigmentation. The before and after ACTH values are high. What is the most likely clinical diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Pituitary adenoma"},{"t":"Ectopic source"},{"t":"Exogenous source"},{"t":"Adrenal adenoma."}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Ectopic source:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"High-dose dexamethasone suppression test is used for etiological diagnosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It helps to differentiate between ectopic ACTH production and pituitary adenoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Increased levels of both before and after indicate ectopic source."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"ACTH has partial melanocyte-stimulating hormone-like action which causes hyperpigmentation on creases of skin (palms and soles)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Carcinoid tumors and Oat cell lung cancer are the two sources of ectopic production of ACTH"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Pituitary adenoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Here both ACTH and cortisol levels are increased."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Exogenous source:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cortisol levels are raised and ACTH is decreased on administering iatrogenic steroids."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Adrenal adenoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In adrenal adenoma, ACTCH and cortisol both are reduced."}]},{"n":43,"q":"In a female patient with bronchial asthma, who arrives at the emergency department experiencing intense restlessness, palpitations, and tremors, an examination reveals a swollen neck. Additionally, her blood pressure is elevated, and tachycardia is observed. The ECG results indicate atrial fibrillation. Which medication should be administered promptly for the initial treatment of this patient?","o":[{"t":"Diltiazem"},{"t":"Propranolol"},{"t":"Esmolol"},{"t":"Propylthiouracil"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Diltiazem"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"According to the vignette, the patient has all signs of a Thyrotoxic crisis- thyroid storm including restlessness, palpitations, and tremors."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Administering diltiazem in a patient with atrial fibrillation triggered by hyperthyroidism is the optimal approach for this patient to ensure hemodynamic stability and better management of thyroid storm with PTU"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B and C"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Beta blocker is the first-line drug to treat tachycardia and palpitations in a patient with a thyrotoxic crisis. However, the patient in the vignette is asthmatic, and beta blockers are contraindicated in asthmatics."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rate control for atrial fibrillation should be done before administration PTU, to manage the thyroid storm."}]},{"n":44,"q":"A patient came to the hospital with complaints of lethargy, increased sleep, and weight gain. Investigations revealed low plasma TSH concentration. However, on the administration of TRH, the TSH levels increased. Which of the conditions is likely in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Hyperthyroidism due to primary thyroid disease"},{"t":"Hypothyroidism due to thyroid gland"},{"t":"Hypothyroidism due to disease in the hypothalamus"},{"t":"Hyperthyroidism due to disease in the pituitary"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option C - Hypothyroidism due to disease in the hypothalamus:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The scenario described in the question is indicative of a dysfunction at the level of the hypothalamus."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When the hypothalamus senses low levels of thyroid hormones (T3 and T4) in the bloodstream, it releases thyrotropin-releasing hormone (TRH). TRH stimulates the anterior pituitary gland to release TSH, which, in turn, stimulates the thyroid gland to produce and release T3 and T4."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In this patient, the hypothalamus is not producing sufficient TRH, leading to decreased stimulation of the pituitary to produce TSH"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Hyperthyroidism due to primary thyroid disease- This is unlikely since the patient's symptoms and the low TSH levels indicate hypothyroidism rather than hyperthyroidism."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Hypothyroidism due to thyroid gland- In this case there is reduced T4 and T3 and increased TSH."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hyperthyroidism due to disease in the pituitary- This is unlikely due to the patient's symptoms and the low TSH levels. Additionally, administration of TRH would not result in increased TSH levels in a hyperthyroid patient."}]},{"n":45,"q":"Gynecomastia results from an imbalance between androgen and oestrogen activity. All of the below are causes of gynecomastia, except.","o":[{"t":"Aromatase inhibitors"},{"t":"Liver failure"},{"t":"Spironolactone"},{"t":"Digoxin"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Aromatase inhibitors:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Gynecomastia refers to the increased development of breast gland tissue in males."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Drugs inducing Gynecomastia Cimetidine Digoxin Anti-androgens (Cyproterone acetate, spirinolactone) Some exogenous anabolic steroids (diethylstilbestrol) Cannabis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cimetidine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Digoxin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-androgens (Cyproterone acetate, spirinolactone)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Some exogenous anabolic steroids (diethylstilbestrol)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cannabis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cimetidine"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Digoxin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-androgens (Cyproterone acetate, spirinolactone)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Some exogenous anabolic steroids (diethylstilbestrol)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cannabis"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Liver diseases can cause increased sexual hormone-binding hormone levels, resulting in the development of breast gland tissue."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Spironolactone causes decreased production of Testosterone, thereby causing Gynecomastia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Digoxin has estrogenic action, contributing to the development of breast gland tissue."}]},{"n":46,"q":"What is the most frequently encountered tumor in the anterior mediastinum?","o":[{"t":"Thymoma"},{"t":"Lymphoma"},{"t":"Adenocarcinoma"},{"t":"Fibroma"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option A - Thymoma:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Thymoma is the most common tumor in the anterior mediastinum. Thymomas are malignant epithelial cell tumors that form in the Thymus. They are commonly found in the perivascular or anterior mediastinum."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Lymphoma refers to the cancerous condition of lymph nodes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Adenocarcinoma is the most common lung cancer."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Fibromas are tumors that form in the uterus. These are noncancerous and often referred to as fibroids."}]},{"n":47,"q":"Which of the microorganisms listed below will exhibit intrinsic resistance to meropenem and aminoglycosides, but have sensitivity to cotrimoxazole?","o":[{"t":"Burkholderia cepacia"},{"t":"Acinetobacter"},{"t":"Pseudomonas"},{"t":"Stenotrophomonas"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D: Stenotrophomonas"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Stenotrophomonas maltophilia, formerly known as Pseudomonas maltophilia is a Gram-negative bacterium and an oppurtunistic pathogen that causes wound infection, UTI and septicemia in helathcare settings."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The organism is sensitive to cotrimoxazole and resistant to carbapenems, and aminoglycosides."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A,B, C: These organisms are susceptible to the drugs mentioned in the question."}]},{"n":48,"q":"Match the following? a. Trisomy 13 1. Huntington disease b. Trisomy 18 2. Patau syndrome c. Trinucleotide repeat sequence 3. Sickle disease d. Hb point mutation of glutamate to valine 4. Edward syndrome\na. Trisomy 13 1. Huntington disease\nb. Trisomy 18 2. Patau syndrome\nc. Trinucleotide repeat sequence 3. Sickle disease\nd. Hb point mutation of glutamate to valine 4. Edward syndrome","o":[{"t":"a2-b4-c1-d3"},{"t":"b1-a3-d2-c4"},{"t":"a2-b1-c4-d3"},{"t":"a4-b1-c3-d2"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - a2-b4-c1-d3:"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"a. Trisomy 13 2. Patau syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"b. Trisomy 18 4. Edward syndrome"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"c. Trinucleotide repeat sequence 1. Huntington disease"},{"k":"p","l":1,"v":"d. Hb point mutation of glutamate to valine 3. Sickle disease"}]},{"n":49,"q":"All of the following are tests done for screening in patients with Turner's syndrome, except?","o":[{"t":"ANA"},{"t":"Audiometry"},{"t":"Echocardiography"},{"t":"Fasting blood glucose"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: ANA (Antinuclear Antibody) test:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The ANA test is not typically performed as part of Turner mosaic screening. ANA testing is primarily used to help diagnose autoimmune conditions such as systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE) or other connective tissue diseases."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Audiometry: Audiometry is a test used to screen for hearing loss at diagnosis and every one to five years thereafter in these patients. There is an association of Turner's syndrome with sensorineural hearing loss, which makes audiometry essential in these patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C: Echocardiography: It is an important component of screening Turner's patients every five to ten years due to their increased risk of various cardiovascular abnormalities like aortic root dilatation. Cardiac MRI has higher sensitivity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Fasting blood glucose: Annual fasting blood glucose is monitored as these patients are at increased risk of developing metabolic diseases including diabetes mellitus."}]},{"n":50,"q":"Which of the following is a characteristic feature of Kallmann syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Anosmia"},{"t":"Syndactyly in males"},{"t":"Precocious puberty in females"},{"t":"White forelock"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Anosmia:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Kallmann syndrome results from the defective hypothalmic gonadotropin-releasing hormone synthesis and is associated with anosmia or hyposmia due to olfactory bulb agenesis or hypoplasia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B, C and D are not characteristic features of Kallman syndrome"}]},{"n":51,"q":"What is the probable diagnosis for a woman who has symptoms of altered mental state, difficulty breathing, low blood pressure, and slow heart rate, along with non-pitting swelling in the limbs? Additionally, she has a long-standing history of weight gain, constipation, sensitivity to cold, and heavy menstrual bleeding.","o":[{"t":"Septic shock"},{"t":"Cardiogenic shock"},{"t":"Myxedema coma"},{"t":"Hyperthyroidism"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Myxedema coma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"When the thyroid gland does not create enough thyroid hormones, the condition is known as hypothyroidism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A complication of severe hypothyroidism is myxedema coma, commonly referred to as myxedema crisis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of altered sensorium, hypotension, bradycardia, long-standing history of weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, menorrhagia, and non-pitting edema are characteristic features of myxedema coma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Septic shock: The patient's long-standing history of weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, and menorrhagia suggests an underlying chronic condition rather than an acute infection."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cardiogenic shock: Cardiogenic shock is characterized by a low cardiac output leading to hypotension, but it does not typically cause altered sensorium or non-pitting edema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hyperthyroidism: Hyperthyroidism is associated with symptoms such as weight loss, heat intolerance, palpitations, and tremors."}]},{"n":52,"q":"In the given sagittal brain section, the colored region corresponds to the paracentral lobule. What structures would be impacted by a lesion in this particular area?","o":[{"t":"Scapular region and neck"},{"t":"Trunk and shoulder"},{"t":"Perineum and leg"},{"t":"Face and neck"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Perineum and leg:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A lesion in the paracentral lobule can affect the motor function of the perineum and legs, causing weakness, paralysis, or spasticity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Other parts of the brain, like the primary motor cortex, premotor cortex, and supplementary motor area in the precentral gyrus and surrounding regions of the cerebral hemisphere, control motor function in the scapular region and neck."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - The motor control of th e trunk and shoulder is administered by different region of the cerebrum, like the essential engine cortex, premotor cortex , and valuable engine region, which are situated in the precentral gyrus and encompassing locales of the cerebral half of the globe."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - The p rimary motor cortex , which is located in the precentral gyrus of the frontal lobe, is primarily in charge of the motor control of the face and neck. Spasticity or weakness of the face and neck can result from lesions in these areas"}],"qi":[5477]},{"n":53,"q":"The patient in question is a 25-year-old male who presented with symptoms of palpitations, sweating, and restlessness. He also mentioned experiencing sweaty palms. The image provided below displays the clinical examination findings. Which diagnostic test is performed on this patient?","o":[{"t":"Anti-thyroglobulin antibody"},{"t":"Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody"},{"t":"Thyroid receptor antibody"},{"t":"Elevated ultrasensitive thyrotropin levels"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Thyroid receptor antibody:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"According to the vignette, the patient suffers from hyperthyroidism symptoms, including palpitations, sweating, restlessness, and sweaty palms. The most common cause of hyperthyroidism is Grave’s disease."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Grave’s thyrotoxicosis results from the production of IgG antibodies directed against TSH receptor on the thyroid follicular cell, which stimulate thyroid hormone production and proliferation of follicular cells ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"These antibodies are termed thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulins or TSH receptor antibodies and are detected in the serum of 80-95% of patients of Grave’s disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A: Anti-thyroglobulin antibody"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is not as sensitive as the thyroid receptor antibody for Grave’s disease and hence, does not need to be tested in the patient in the vignette."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B: Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-thyroid peroxidase antibody is extremely specific for the presence of Hashimoto’s thyroiditis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D: Elevated ultrasensitive thyrotropin levels"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated ultrasensitive thyrotropin levels, or TSH levels are seen in cases of hypothyroidism."}],"qi":[5480]},{"n":54,"q":"Which of the subsequent manifestations is observed in pituitary apoplexy?","o":[{"t":"Hypertension"},{"t":"Shock"},{"t":"Unconsciousness"},{"t":"Fatigue"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Shock:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pituitary apoplexy refers to the sudden hemorrhage or infarction of the pituitary gland, typically occurring in the presence of a pre-existing pituitary adenoma."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In pituitary apoplexy, bleeding or infarction within the pituitary gland can lead to the release of vasoactive substances and cause a sudden drop in blood pressure."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This can result in shock, which is characterized by decreased blood perfusion to vital organs and subsequent organ dysfunction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"OptionA - Hypertension: Hypertension, or high blood pressure, is not a typical finding in pituitary apoplexy. Instead, pituitary apoplexy can cause a drop in blood pressure due to the development of shock, as discussed below."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Unconsciousness: Pituitary apoplexy can cause varying degrees of neurological symptoms, including headache, visual disturbances, and altered consciousness. However, the development of unconsciousness may not be a specific or universal feature in all cases."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Fatigue: Fatigue is a non-specific symptom and is not typically a prominent feature of pituitary apoplexy. Other symptoms, such as headache, visual changes, and hormonal disturbances, may be more characteristic."}]},{"n":55,"q":"Which type of neuroendocrine tumor could potentially cause these symptoms in a 49-year-old male who is experiencing recurring episodes of watery diarrhea, dehydration, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria?","o":[{"t":"Insulinoma"},{"t":"Somatostatinoma"},{"t":"VIPoma"},{"t":"Glucagonoma"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - VIPoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"VIPoma, which stands for vasoactive intestinal peptide-secreting tumor , is the neuroendocrine tumor that can be the cause of recurrent episodes of watery diarrhea, dehydration, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The WDHA syndrome , which includes"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Watery diarrhea"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Dehydration"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Hypokalemia"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Achlorhydria, can be brought on by excessive VIP secretion."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Choices:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Insulinoma- Insulinoma can cause weakness, fatigue, sweating, palpitations, confusion, seizures , and other symptoms"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Somatostatinoma- Nonspecific symptoms of somatostatinoma include abdominal pain, diabetes mellitus, cholelithiasis, diarrhea, steatorrhea, and weight loss. Somatostatin inhibits the digestive enzymes' secretion and gastrointestinal tract motility, resulting in diarrhea."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Glucagonoma- A rare neuroendocrine tumor of the pancreatic alpha cells that secretes an excessive amount of glucagon,Hyperglycemia , a rash on the skin, a decrease in weight , and anemia are all possible signs of glucagonoma."}]},{"n":56,"q":"A 30-year-old female patient presented with bone pain and abdominal cramps. Her family reported a previous history of abnormal behavior. The consultant doctor made a tentative diagnosis based on the clinical symptoms. What would be the most appropriate investigative method to establish a conclusive diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"MRI"},{"t":"Ultrasonogram"},{"t":"Sestamibi scan"},{"t":"CT scan"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Sestamibi scan:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The presence of bone pain, abdominal cramps, and a history of abnormal behaviour point towards primary hyperparathyroidism."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Most common cause is parathyroid adenoma and therefore sestamibi scan is best answer here."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options: Options A, B, and D are incorrect."}]},{"n":57,"q":"In a 60-year-old male, what is the primary etiology typically associated with Cushing's disease?","o":[{"t":"Small-cell lung cancer"},{"t":"Adrenal tumor"},{"t":"Ectopic ACTH syndrome"},{"t":"Pituitary microadenoma"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Pituitary microadenoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pituitary microadenoma is the most common cause of Cushing's disease in a 60-year-old male."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cushing's disease is a type of hypercortisolism caused by an ACTH-secreting pituitary adenoma (a benign tumour in the pituitary gland)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This type of tumor overproduces ACTH, which stimulates the adrenal glands to produce excessive cortisol."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Choices:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Small-cell lung cancer"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Certain cancers, most notably small-cell lung cancer, can produce ACTH or ACTH-like substances, resulting in Cushing's syndrome . However, especially in this age group, this is not the most common cause of Cushing's disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Adrenal tumor"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An adrenal tumor, specifically an adrenal adenoma or adrenal carcinoma, can produce cortisol on its own, resulting in Cushing's syndrome . However, as previously stated, the primary cause of Cushing's disease is a pituitary microadenoma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Ectopic ACTH syndrome"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ectopic ACTH syndrome occurs when a non-pituitary tumor (e.g., lung, pancreas, or thymus) produces ACTH, resulting in Cushing's syndrome. While this can be a cause of Cushing's syndrome, it is not the most common cause of Cushing's disease in a 60-year-old male."}]},{"n":58,"q":"A child was to brought to the ER in an unconcious state after severe vomiting and abdominal pain. His blood glucose was 370 mg/dl and ketones 3+. What is the leading cause of death in diabetic ketoacidosis?","o":[{"t":"Acute respiratory distress syndrome"},{"t":"Cardiac arrhythmia"},{"t":"Cerebral edema"},{"t":"Acute kidney injury"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Cerebral edema:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Leading cause of death in a child is cerebral edema."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Due to the damage to the BBB > dehydration > infection."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS): ARDS is one of the leading cause of death in adults with DKA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cardiac arrhythmia: DKA can lead to electrolyte imbalances, including abnormalities in potassium levels, which can contribute to cardiac arrhythmias, they are not the most common cause of mortality."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Acute kidney injury (AKI): AKI can occur in the setting of DKA, but it is not the most common cause of mortality. DKA can lead to dehydration and hypoperfusion, possibly contributing to kidney injury."}]},{"n":59,"q":"A patient presents with complaints of sudden onset headache, palpitation, and profuse sweating. The patient has had multiple similar episodes in the past. During these episodes, the patient's blood pressure is elevated. A 24-hour urine metanephrine is elevated. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Addison's disease"},{"t":"Renal artery stenosis"},{"t":"Pheochromocytoma"},{"t":"Hyperthyroidism"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Correct option C - Pheochromocytoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pheochromocytoma produces excessive amounts of catecholamines , leading to symptoms like sudden onset headache, palpitation and profuse sweating."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The diagnosis is confirmed by measuring elevated levels of urinary metanephrines."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"A 24-hour urine metanephrine test is a diagnostic test for pheochromocytoma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Addison's disease: It presents with symptoms like fatigue, weight loss, low blood pressure, and skin hyperpigmentation . While adrenal crisis can cause symptoms such as headache and sweating, it does not cause an episodic elevation of blood pressure."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Renal artery stenosis : It can lead to high blood pressure but does not cause sudden onset headaches, palpitation, and profuse sweating. An elevated 24-hour urine metanephrine is not associated with renal artery stenosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hyperthyroidism: Hyperthyroidism symptoms include weight loss, increased appetite, tremors, heat intolerance, palpitations, and sweating . An elevated 24-hour urinary metanephrine is not associated with hyperthyroidism."}]},{"n":60,"q":"What drug is recommended for the treatment of a 29-year-old male patient who has been experiencing coarse facial features and gradual enlargement of the hands and feet for the past decade, and has been diagnosed with acromegaly based on elevated levels of IGF-1 and growth hormone that did not suppress after a 75 g glucose challenge test?","o":[{"t":"Lanreotide depot formulation"},{"t":"Terlipressin"},{"t":"Ketoconazole"},{"t":"Leuprolide"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Lanreotide depot formulation:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Lanreotide is a somatostatin analog that inhibits the release of GH."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is administered as a long-acting depot injection."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Considered the preferred medical therapy for acromegaly due to its high efficacy in controlling GH and IGF-1 levels, as well as its long duration of action."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Choices:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Terlipressin is a synthetic vasopressin analog used primarily for the treatment of hepatorenal syndrome and bleeding esophageal varices"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Ketoconazol is antifungal medication that inhibits the synthesis of adrenal and gonadal steroids. It has been used off-label for the medical management of acromegaly in the past"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Leuprolide is a gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) agonist commonly used for the treatment of conditions such as prostate cancer and endometriosis."}]},{"n":61,"q":"A 25-year-old P1L1 woman is planning for her second child. She has been recently diagnosed with Grave’s disease. Which of the following is the antithyroid drug to be used in early gestation?","o":[{"t":"Propylthiouracil"},{"t":"Methimazole"},{"t":"Carbimazole"},{"t":"All the above"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Propylthiouracil:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Grave's disease is an autoimmune disorder that causes hyperthyroidism, which results in the overproduction of thyroid hormones by the thyroid gland."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Propylthiouracil (PTU) is an antithyroid drug that works by inhibiting the production of thyroid hormones. PTU is the preferred drug for use during early pregnancy, particularly in the first trimester, as it has a lower risk of causing birth defects compared to Methimazole."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect choices:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Methimazole is also an antithyroid drug that works by inhibiting the production of thyroid hormones. Methimazole is usually avoided during the first trimester of pregnancy due to its potential risk of causing birth defects. However, it can be used during the second and third trimesters of pregnancy if PTU is not well-tolerated."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Carbimazole is a prodrug of Methimazole. Like Methimazole, it is avoided during the first trimester of pregnancy due to the risk of causing birth defects."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Propylthiouracil can be used in early gestation, but Methimazole and Carbimazole are avoided during the first trimester of the pregnancy."}]},{"n":62,"q":"What should be done next in the management of a patient who is diabetic and presents with symptoms of vomiting and abdominal pain? Upon examination, the patient displays hyperventilation, sweet breath, and low blood pressure. The blood glucose level is 320 mg/dL, and urine ketones are detected.","o":[{"t":"IV insulin drip"},{"t":"IV soda-bicarbonate"},{"t":"Check blood ketone levels"},{"t":"IVF and check K+"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - IVF and check K+:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient in the given scenario presents with symptoms of diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA), a potentially life-threatening complication of diabetes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with DKA are typically dehydrated due to excessive urination and vomiting, and they also lose potassium in the urine. Hypokalemia (low potassium levels) can occur due to insulin therapy, so it is important to check and monitor potassium levels closely during treatment."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Choices:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - IV insulin drip is a necessary treatment for DKA. Still, it should be initiated after fluid resuscitation to avoid causing a sudden drop in blood glucose levels, which can lead to cerebral edema."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - IV soda-bicarbonate is not recommended for the initial management of DKA as it may worsen the acidosis by causing a shift in the acid-base balance."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - While checking blood ketone levels is important for diagnosing DKA, it does not address the acute management of the condition."}]},{"n":63,"q":"What should be the next course of action in managing a patient who has had diabetes mellitus for the last 5 years and is currently experiencing vomiting and abdominal pain? The patient is not adhering to medication and shows signs of dehydration. Diagnostic tests indicate a blood sugar level of 500 mg/dl and the presence of ketone bodies.","o":[{"t":"Intravenous fluids with long-acting insulin"},{"t":"Intravenous fluids"},{"t":"Intravenous insulin"},{"t":"Intravenous fluids followed by regular insulin"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Intravenous fluids followed by regular insulin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DKA is characterized by hyperglycemia (blood glucose >250 mg/dL), ketosis (presence of ketone bodies in blood/urine), and metabolic acidosis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Symptoms include vomiting, abdominal pain, dehydration, and altered mental status."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient has a blood glucose level of 500 mg/dL, presence of ketone bodies, and signs of dehydration, which are all indicative of DKA."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Management of DKA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Intravenous Fluids : Initial Step : Start with 0.9% normal saline to correct dehydration and restore intravascular volume. Fluid Resuscitation : Typically, 1-2 liters of normal saline are given in the first hour, followed by a more gradual replacement based on the patient's hemodynamic status and urine output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initial Step : Start with 0.9% normal saline to correct dehydration and restore intravascular volume."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid Resuscitation : Typically, 1-2 liters of normal saline are given in the first hour, followed by a more gradual replacement based on the patient's hemodynamic status and urine output."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin Therapy : Regular Insulin : Administered as an intravenous infusion. Start with a bolus of regular insulin (0.1 units/kg), followed by a continuous infusion (0.1 units/kg/hr) to reduce blood glucose levels and suppress ketogenesis. Monitoring : Blood glucose levels should be monitored hourly. Once the blood glucose falls below 200-250 mg/dL, the infusion rate is adjusted, and dextrose may be added to the IV fluids to prevent hypoglycemia while continuing to clear ketones."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular Insulin : Administered as an intravenous infusion. Start with a bolus of regular insulin (0.1 units/kg), followed by a continuous infusion (0.1 units/kg/hr) to reduce blood glucose levels and suppress ketogenesis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monitoring : Blood glucose levels should be monitored hourly. Once the blood glucose falls below 200-250 mg/dL, the infusion rate is adjusted, and dextrose may be added to the IV fluids to prevent hypoglycemia while continuing to clear ketones."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Electrolyte Management : Potassium : Monitor and replace potassium as needed, since insulin administration and fluid resuscitation can drive potassium into cells, leading to hypokalemia."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Potassium : Monitor and replace potassium as needed, since insulin administration and fluid resuscitation can drive potassium into cells, leading to hypokalemia."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Intravenous Fluids :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Initial Step : Start with 0.9% normal saline to correct dehydration and restore intravascular volume."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fluid Resuscitation : Typically, 1-2 liters of normal saline are given in the first hour, followed by a more gradual replacement based on the patient's hemodynamic status and urine output."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Insulin Therapy :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Regular Insulin : Administered as an intravenous infusion. Start with a bolus of regular insulin (0.1 units/kg), followed by a continuous infusion (0.1 units/kg/hr) to reduce blood glucose levels and suppress ketogenesis."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Monitoring : Blood glucose levels should be monitored hourly. Once the blood glucose falls below 200-250 mg/dL, the infusion rate is adjusted, and dextrose may be added to the IV fluids to prevent hypoglycemia while continuing to clear ketones."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Electrolyte Management :"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Potassium : Monitor and replace potassium as needed, since insulin administration and fluid resuscitation can drive potassium into cells, leading to hypokalemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Intravenous fluids with long-acting insulin: Long-acting insulin isn't fitting for the treatment of DKA, because it does not reduce blood glucose levels rapidly enough to treat the acute hyperglycemia related to DKA."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Intravenous fluids alone: Whereas intravenous liquids are a critical component of DKA management, insulin treatment is essential to reduce blood glucose levels and ketone bodies. Without insulin therapy, the patient's acidosis and electrolyte imbalances will not improve."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Intravenous insulin alone : Whereas insulin treatment is vital for DKA management, intravenous fluids are moreover required to correct liquid and electrolyte imbalances. Without intravenous fluids, the persistent may develop hypotension and declining electrolyte imbalances."}]},{"n":64,"q":"Which prolactin levels are indicative of prolactinoma?","o":[{"t":">50µg/L"},{"t":">100µg/L"},{"t":">150µg/L"},{"t":">200µg/L"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option: D >200µg/L"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Elevated prolactin levels are a hallmark feature of prolactinomas, and levels exceeding 200µg/L are considered significantly elevated and strongly indicative of a prolactinoma."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A, B, and C: These statements are incorrect."}]},{"n":65,"q":"Which of the following joints are commonly involved in diabetes? MC joint involved in diabetes is?","o":[{"t":"Metatarsophalangeal joints"},{"t":"Knee"},{"t":"Shoulder"},{"t":"Charcot"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Metatarsophalangeal joints:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In diabetes, one of the commonly affected joints is the Charcot joint, particularly the metatarsophalangeal (MTP) joints ."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Charcot joint mainly involves the foot is frequently affected in individuals with diabetes."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Complications such as peripheral neuropathy, and poor circulation (peripheral arterial disease) can lead to foot-related issues such as ulcers, infections, and joint deformities."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Ankle- While complications of diabetes can affect the ankle joint indirectly, such as peripheral neuropathy leading to instability and increased risk of ankle injuries, the ankle joint itself is not a primary site of involvement in diabetes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Knee- Diabetes can lead to various complications and conditions that may affect the knee joint indirectly, such as diabetic neuropathy or diabetic arthropathy (Charcot joint), but the knee joint itself is not a primary site of involvement."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Shoulder- Shoulder joint involvement is not commonly associated with diabetes. Diabetes-related joint problems typically manifest in weight-bearing joints and those subjected to excessive pressure, such as the foot."}]},{"n":66,"q":"What is the most frequent cause of fulminant diabetes?\n(or)\nMost common cause of fulminant diabetes is?","o":[{"t":"Viruses"},{"t":"Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"t":"Non-ketotic hyperosmolar coma"},{"t":"Autoimmunity"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Viruses:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fulminant diabetes, also known as fulminant type 1 diabetes or acute-onset type 1 diabetes , is a rare and rapidly progressing form of diabetes characterized by severe hyperglycemia and a rapid loss of insulin-producing beta cells in the pancreas."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Viruses, particularly enteroviruses such as Coxsackie B virus, have been implicated as a potential cause of fulminant diabetes . These viruses can infect and damage the pancreatic beta cells, leading to their destruction and subsequent loss of insulin production."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B -Diabetic Ketoacidosis"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"DKA occurs primarily in individuals with established diabetes, particularly in type 1 diabetes, and is characterized by the presence of hyperglycemia, ketosis, and acidosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Non-ketotic Hyperosmolar Coma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Non-ketotic hyperosmolar coma is characterized by extremely high blood glucose levels and severe dehydration. However, it is not the most common cause of fulminant diabetes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Autoimmunity"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the case of fulminant diabetes, the rapid onset and progression of the disease suggest a different underlying cause, such as viral infection, rather than the gradual autoimmune destruction of beta cells seen in typical type 1 diabetes."}]},{"n":67,"q":"In a medical camp established on a military base, a 20-year-old military recruit is discovered to have an HbA1C level of 6.1% and a fasting blood glucose of 120 mg/dL. The individual does not have a familial background of diabetes mellitus and displays no indications or symptoms of diabetes. The general physical examination shows no noteworthy findings. Which of the following options most accurately characterizes the blood glucose levels in this patient?","o":[{"t":"Normal"},{"t":"Impaired Glucose tolerance"},{"t":"Diabetes Mellitus"},{"t":"Maturity onset diabetes in Young"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Impaired Glucose tolerance:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The patient in the scenario has an HbA1C level of 6.1% and a fasting blood glucose level of 120 mg/dL . These values fall within the range that indicates impaired glucose tolerance (IGT)."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Impaired glucose tolerance (IGT) is a condition in which blood glucose levels are higher than normal but not high enough to be classified as diabetes mellitus ."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Normal- Normal blood glucose levels typically fall within a range of 70-99 mg/dL for fasting blood glucose and below 5.7% for HbA1C."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Diabetes Mellitus- Diabetes mellitus is diagnosed when fasting blood glucose levels are consistently above 126 mg/dL or HbA1C levels are equal to or higher than 6.5%."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Maturity onset diabetes in Young (MODY)- Maturity onset diabetes of the young (MODY) is a type of diabetes characterized by a strong family history, early onset before the age of 25, and a specific genetic etiology."}]},{"n":68,"q":"During her pregnancy, a woman who had Grave's disease was consistently taking antithyroid medication. Consequently, the newborn suffered from aplasia cutis congenita. Which specific medication could be attributed to the occurrence of this condition?","o":[{"t":"Carbimazole"},{"t":"Levothyroxine"},{"t":"Methylthiouracil"},{"t":"Hydrouracil"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Carbimazole:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Aplasia cutis congenita is a rare condition characterized by the absence of skin in localized or widespread areas at birth."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Carbimazole is contraindicated in 1st trimester of pregnancy due to its teratogenic effects"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Levothyroxine: Levothyroxine is a synthetic thyroid hormone used to treat hypothyroidism. It is not associated with an increased risk of aplasia cutis congenita."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Methylthiouracil: There is no significant evidence linking methylthiouracil to an increased risk of aplasia cutis congenita."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hydrouracil: Hydrouracil is not a commonly used antithyroid medication. It is not associated with an increased risk of aplasia cutis congenita."}]},{"n":69,"q":"A diabetic patent walks into your clinic and does “namaste”. The image is given below. What could be the probable cause?","o":[{"t":"Flexor tenosynovitis"},{"t":"Cheiroarthropathy"},{"t":"Dupuytrens contracture"},{"t":"Ankyloses"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Cheiroarthropathy:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Cheiroarthropathy is thought to be related to long-standing poor glycemic control and the accumulation of advanced glycation end-products (AGEs) in the skin and soft tissues."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"This condition can cause difficulty in fully extending the fingers, leading to a characteristic hand posture resembling the \"namaste\" gesture."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Flexor tenosynovitis: While flexor tenosynovitis can occur in individuals with diabetes, it does not specifically explain the \"namaste\" gesture mentioned in the question."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Dupuytren's contracture: Although Dupuytren's contracture is associated with certain risk factors, such as family history and alcoholism, it is not directly related to the \"namaste\" gesture mentioned in the question."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Ankyloses: While ankylosis can occur in various conditions, including certain forms of arthritis, trauma, or infection, it does not specifically explain the \"namaste\" gesture in the context of a diabetic patient."}],"qi":[5491]},{"n":70,"q":"Which of the following statements correctly describes an ideal candidate for a renal graft transplant in a patient with diabetic nephropathy?","o":[{"t":"The survival rate of graft is 95% in the first year"},{"t":"The transplantation is cost effective after the second transplant year"},{"t":"The life expectancy is doubled in a diabetic patient with renal transplant"},{"t":"The treatment of chronic rejection has improved over the last 10 years"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - The transplantation is cost effective after the second transplant year:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Renal transplantation is considered the treatment of choice for end-stage renal disease (ESRD) in patients with diabetic nephropathy with eGFR <20 ml/min."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - \"The survival rate of the graft is 95% in the first year,\" is an overgeneralization. While the survival rate of renal grafts in the first year can be relatively high, it is not consistently 95% for all patients."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - \"The life expectancy is doubled in a diabetic patient with renal transplant,\" is not entirely accurate. While renal transplantation can improve the quality of life and increase life expectancy in patients with ESRD, it does not necessarily double the life expectancy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - \"The treatment of chronic rejection has improved over the last 10 years,\" is not directly addressed in the given statement. Chronic rejection remains a challenge in renal transplantation."}]},{"n":71,"q":"A patient presented with chronic diarrhea and steatorrhea. D-xylose test was normal and the Schilling test was abnormal. A duodenal biopsy was normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Celiac disease"},{"t":"Ulcerative colitis"},{"t":"Intestinal lymphangiectasia"},{"t":"Pancreatic insufficiency"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - Pancreatic insufficiency:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Pancreatic insufficiency refers to the inadequate production or secretion of pancreatic enzymes, including lipase, amylase, and proteases. This can lead to malabsorption of nutrients, particularly fats, resulting in chronic diarrhea and steatorrhea (excessive fat in the stool). In this case, the patient's symptoms of chronic diarrhea and steatorrhea suggest impaired fat digestion and absorption."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The D-xylose test is used to assess the absorption of xylose, a sugar, in the small intestine and an abnormal Schilling test result suggests impaired absorption of vitamin B12."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"An abnormal Schilling test result suggests impaired absorption of vitamin B12."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options :"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Celiac disease: Celiac disease is an autoimmune condition characterized by an abnormal immune response to gluten, leading to damage to the small intestine."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Ulcerative colitis: Ulcerative colitis is a form of inflammatory bowel disease primarily affecting the colon and rectum."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Intestinal lymphangiectasia: Intestinal lymphangiectasia is a condition characterized by dilated lymphatic vessels in the intestinal wall, leading to the leakage of lymphatic fluid into the intestinal lumen."}]},{"n":72,"q":"A middle-aged woman with a history of constipation, dry skin and menorrhagia presents to the ER with altered sensorium, non-pitting edema, hypothermia, bradycardia, and hypotension. What is the most likely diagnosis?","o":[{"t":"Septic shock"},{"t":"Cardiogenic shock"},{"t":"Myxedema coma"},{"t":"Hypoglycemia"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Myxedema coma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Myxedema coma is life threatening complication of hypothyroidism that is triggered by UTI, MI, Stroke, CHF, abuse of sedatives."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Patients with myxedema coma present with altered mental status, hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension, and signs of systemic decompensation. The symptoms of constipation, dry skin, and menorrhagia are also commonly associated with hypothyroidism."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Septic shock: Septic shock is a life-threatening condition caused by a severe infection leading to systemic inflammation and organ dysfunction."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Cardiogenic shock: Cardiogenic shock occurs when the heart's ability to pump blood is severely compromised, leading to inadequate tissue perfusion."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Hypoglycemia: Hypoglycemia, or low blood sugar, can cause altered mental status and hypotension."}]},{"n":73,"q":"What is the most probable cause of hypokalemia, hypertension, and metabolic alkalosis in a 35-year-old female patient?","o":[{"t":"Bartter syndrome"},{"t":"Gitelman’s syndrome"},{"t":"Liddle’s syndrome"},{"t":"Fanconi’s syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Liddle’s syndrome:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Liddle's syndrome is an autosomal dominant disease caused by a defect in epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) exhibiting a gain of function."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Liddle's syndrome is characterized by hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A- Bartter syndrome: Bartter syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that affects the loop of Henle in the kidney and leads to salt wasting, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B- Gitelman's syndrome: Gitelman's syndrome is another genetic disorder that affects the distal convoluted tubules of the kidney and results in hypokalemia, metabolic alkalosis, and hypomagnesemia."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D- Fanconi's syndrome: Fanconi's syndrome is a condition characterized by dysfunction of the proximal tubules of the kidney, leading to hypokalemia, metabolic acidosis, and other electrolyte abnormalities can occur."}]},{"n":74,"q":"Which of the subsequent is absent in MEN 2B syndrome?","o":[{"t":"Megacolon"},{"t":"Parathyroid adenoma"},{"t":"Mucosal neuroma"},{"t":"Marfanoid habitus"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Parathyroid adenoma:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Parathyroid adenoma is not visualized in MEN 2B syndrome."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"It is a common finding in MEN 1 syndrome, MEN 1, that occurs due to mutations in the MEN1 gene."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":""},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Megacolon"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Megacolon is a feature of Hirschsprung's disease and is associated with accompanying MEN 2B syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Mucosal neuroma"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Mucosal neuromas are a hallmark of MEN 2B syndrome and are visualized in the oral mucosa, conjunctiva, and gastrointestinal tract."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Marfanoid habitus"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Marfanoid habitus is visualized in MEN 2B syndrome characterized by exaggerated stature, thin long fingers, and hypermobility of joints."}]},{"n":75,"q":"A 42-year-old patient presents with complaints of bone pain in the hands, difficulty in passing stools, along with history of recurrent renal stones. On investigation, his serum calcium was increased, decreased serum phosphate and X-ray showing subperiosteal resorption of phalanges. In which of the following conditions calcitonin levels are increased?","o":[{"t":"Hyperthyroidism"},{"t":"Hypoparathyroidism"},{"t":"Hyperparathyroidism"},{"t":"Cushing’s syndrome"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - Hyperparathyroidism:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Calcitonin is a hormone produced by the parafollicular or C-cells of the thyroid gland. Its primary function is to regulate calcium and phosphate levels in the body."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In hyperparathyroidism , there is excessive production of parathyroid hormone (PTH) by the parathyroid glands . This condition leads to increased levels of PTH, which in turn can stimulate the production of calcitonin by the thyroid gland."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hyperthyroidism (option A) is characterized by an overactive thyroid gland and is not directly associated with increased calcitonin levels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Hypoparathyroidism (option B) is a condition where there is an underproduction of parathyroid hormone, leading to decreased levels of PTH and subsequently decreased calcitonin levels."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Cushing's syndrome (option D) is a disorder caused by prolonged exposure to high levels of cortisol hormone, typically due to adrenal gland dysfunction. It is not directly associated with changes in calcitonin levels."}]},{"n":76,"q":"In obesity, there is an increase in the production of which of the following hormones?\n(or)\nThe production of which of the following hormones is increased in obesity?","o":[{"t":"Insulin"},{"t":"Thyroxine"},{"t":"Growth hormone"},{"t":"Adiponectin"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Insulin:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Insulin : Obesity is associated with insulin resistance, a condition in which the body's cells become less responsive to the effects of insulin . In response to insulin resistance, the pancreas produces and releases more insulin to compensate for the decreased effectiveness. This increased insulin production is a characteristic feature of obesity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Thyroxine (T4)"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyroid hormones, including thyroxine, are involved in regulating metabolism. While obesity can affect thyroid function, it typically does not cause a direct increase in thyroxine production."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Growth hormone"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Growth hormone (GH) levels may be altered in obesity, but the relationship is complex. In some cases, obese individuals may have reduced GH secretion or impaired response to GH."},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"However, it is not a consistent finding that GH production is increased in obesity."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Adiponectin"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Adiponectin is an adipokine, a hormone secreted by adipose tissue. Its production is generally decreased in obesity, rather than increased. Lower levels of adiponectin are associated with insulin resistance and other metabolic abnormalities seen in obesity."}]},{"n":77,"q":"What is the most frequently observed delayed symptom of radiation therapy for the head and neck?","o":[{"t":"Dysphagia"},{"t":"Xerostomia"},{"t":"Myelopathy"},{"t":"Dysgeusia"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Xerostomia:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The correct answer is \"Xerostomia.\""},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Xerostomia: Xerostomia, also known as dry mouth, is the most common delayed symptom of head and neck radiation therapy. Radiation to the head and neck area can damage the salivary glands, leading to decreased saliva production and resulting in dry mouth."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Dysphagia: Dysphagia refers to difficulty in swallowing and can be a common side effect of head and neck radiation therapy. However, it is not the most common delayed symptom."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Myelopathy: Myelopathy refers to a condition affecting the spinal cord and is not a common delayed symptom of head and neck radiation therapy."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Dysgeusia: Dysgeusia refers to a distorted or altered sense of taste. While taste changes can occur after head and neck radiation therapy, xerostomia is more commonly observed as a delayed symptom."}]},{"n":78,"q":"Which initial screening tests are employed in a primary healthcare center (PHC) for diagnosing diabetes? HbA1c >6.5 Fasting blood sugar >126mg/dl Random blood sugar >200mg/dl 2-hour post glucose load blood sugar","o":[{"t":"1 and 2"},{"t":"2 and 3"},{"t":"2 and 4"},{"t":"1,2 and 3"}],"a":[2],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option C - 2 and 4:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In a primary healthcare (PHC) setting, the initial screening tests used to diagnose diabetes includes FBS ( fasting blood sugar) >126mg/dl and 2-hour post-glucose load blood sugar, which is also known as an oral glucose tolerance test (OGTT as these two tests are less timetaking and can be done at any PHC."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"Options A, B, and D"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"HbA1c >6.5: HbA1c (glycated hemoglobin) level greater than 6.5% is the marker to diagnose diabetes but the process of checking the values of HbA1c is time taking and costly than the FBS and PPBS. Therefore option A cannot be considered correct."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Random blood sugar >200mg/dl: Random blood sugar ( RBS) levels may fluctuate throughout the day and can be influenced by recent meals, making it less suitable for screening purposes."}]},{"n":79,"q":"What is the association of Rhino-cerebral Mucor mycosis?","o":[{"t":"Diabetic ketoacidosis"},{"t":"Pregnancy"},{"t":"Antibiotics"},{"t":"Inhaled steroids"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Diabetic ketoacidosis:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Rhino-cerebral mucormycosis, also known as zygomycosis, is a serious fungal infection caused by various species of fungi belonging to the class Mucorales. It primarily affects the nose, sinuses, and brain."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA): Rhino-cerebral mucormycosis is strongly associated with uncontrolled diabetes, particularly diabetic ketoacidosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Pregnancy: Although pregnancy can cause immunological changes, it is not directly associated with an increased risk of rhino-cerebral mucormycosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Antibiotics: While the use of antibiotics can disrupt the normal microbial balance in the body and potentially lead to fungal infections, they are not specifically associated with rhino-cerebral mucormycosis."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Inhaled steroids: The use of inhaled steroids, such as for asthma or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD), is not directly associated with an increased risk of rhino-cerebral mucormycosis."}]},{"n":80,"q":"What investigations are necessary for a 30-year-old female patient presenting with neck swelling, weight loss, palpitations, exophthalmos, and a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland in the outpatient department? 1. Thyroid scan 2. Fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC) 3. Thyroid function tests 4. Ultrasound of neck 5. Anti-thyroid antibody","o":[{"t":"1,2,3,4 and 5"},{"t":"1,3,5 only"},{"t":"3,4,5 only"},{"t":"1,3,4 and 5 only"}],"a":[3],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option D - 1,3,4 and 5 only:"},{"k":"p","l":0,"v":"The essential investigations for a patient with a diffusely enlarged thyroid gland, exophthalmos, weight loss, and palpitations suggestive of hyperthyroidism or Graves' disease include:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyroid scan: In Graves' disease, the thyroid gland is typically hyperactive and takes up an excessive amount of radioactive iodine."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Thyroid function tests: In Graves' disease, there is typically a low TSH level and elevated levels of T3 and T4."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Ultrasound of the neck: A neck ultrasound is valuable for assessing the size, shape, and characteristics of the thyroid gland."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Anti-thyroid antibodies: In Graves' disease, the presence of antibodies such as anti-thyroid peroxidase antibodies and anti-thyroglobulin antibodies help confirm the autoimmune nature of the disease."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Option A, B, C:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Fine needle aspiration cytology (FNAC): FNAC may not be immediately necessary in this case because the presentation strongly suggests Graves' disease, an autoimmune condition. However, FNAC is more commonly used to evaluate thyroid nodules when malignancy is suspected."}]},{"n":81,"q":"What substances found in urine are utilized as a screening tool for pheochromocytoma?","o":[{"t":"Urinary metanephrines and VMA"},{"t":"Albumin"},{"t":"5-HIAA"},{"t":"Glucose"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Urinary metanephrines and VMA:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Urinary metanephrine and VMA are analysed for 24 hours in urine for diagnosis of pheochromocytoma which is a tumor of the adrenal Medulla also called chromaffinoma which produces norepinephrine and epinephrine."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Albumin, here the 24 hour Urinary Albumin Analysis is done to evaluate Proteinuria which is done in cases to evaluate Renal Disease especially nephrotic syndrome."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - 5-HIAA is serotonin, the 24 hour urinary evaluation for serotonin is done in case of carcinoid tumors."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Glucose is to analyze the level of glucose in urine, mainly for diabetic patients."}]},{"n":82,"q":"What would be the most appropriate next step in managing a 40-year-old female patient who is experiencing cold intolerance, dry skin, constipation, and has a slow ankle jerk on examination, with a TSH level of 15 IU/L and low T3 and T4 levels?","o":[{"t":"Begin treatment with levothyroxine (LT4)"},{"t":"Measure thyroxine binding globulin (TBG)"},{"t":"Order a thyroid radionuclide uptake and scan"},{"t":"Order thyroid ultrasonography"}],"a":[0],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option A - Begin treatment with levothyroxine (LT4):"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"The above case is that of hypothyroidism as there are low levels of total and free T3 and T4 with high TSH."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Treatment of hypothyroidism Levothyroxine- 1.6 µg/kg to be started Available as 25 µg / 50 µg / 88 µg / 100 µg If the dose is >100µg, it increases O2 consumption fi Resulting in Angina. Hence, careful titration of dose increments should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Levothyroxine- 1.6 µg/kg to be started"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Available as 25 µg / 50 µg / 88 µg / 100 µg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If the dose is >100µg, it increases O2 consumption fi Resulting in Angina. Hence, careful titration of dose increments should be done."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Levothyroxine- 1.6 µg/kg to be started"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"Available as 25 µg / 50 µg / 88 µg / 100 µg"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"If the dose is >100µg, it increases O2 consumption fi Resulting in Angina. Hence, careful titration of dose increments should be done."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options:"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option B - Measurement of TBG is not the next best step in case of hypothyroidism"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Thyroid radionuclide uptake and scan is not the next best step."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - A thyroid ultrasound is not the next best step in a case of hypothyroidism."}]},{"n":83,"q":"Which conditions are caused by a mutation in the gene for the aquaporin channel?","o":[{"t":"Liddle’s syndrome"},{"t":"Nephrogenic DI"},{"t":"Cystic fibrosis"},{"t":"Barter syndrome"}],"a":[1],"s":[{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Correct Option B - Nephrogenic DI:"},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"In the case of Nephrogenic DI, there is a mutation in the gene for aquaporin channels, specifically aquaporin-2 (AQP2). This mutation impairs the function of AQP2 channels in the collecting ducts of the kidney, leading to an inability of the kidneys to concentrate urine and reabsorb water properly."},{"k":"b","l":0,"v":"As a result, individuals with Nephrogenic DI experience excessive thirst and polyuria (excessive urine production), as the kidneys are unable to properly concentrate the urine and retain water."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Incorrect Options"},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option A - Liddle's syndrome caused by mutations in the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) genes."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option C - Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in the cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene."},{"k":"h","l":0,"v":"Option D - Barter syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that affects the renal tubules and leads to abnormal salt and water reabsorption, resulting in electrolyte imbalances and excessive urine production."}]}]);
QB.imgs({"5477":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5480":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5455":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5459":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","5491":"data:image/jpeg;base64,"});